   {
    "id": "i10",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     158,
     177
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests that six leading dollar signs before a variable expression reduce to four dollar signs with the variable left unresolved. <p>The substitutor treats {@code $$} as an escape sequence that produces a literal {@code $}. When six dollar signs precede a variable expression, they are parsed as three {@code $$} pairs. The first two pairs each collapse to one {@code $} (yielding {@code $$}), and the third pair escapes the {@code $} that would otherwise start the variable prefix, leaving {@code ${var}} as a literal string instead of being substituted. The net result is four literal dollar signs followed by the unresolved variable expression.</p> <p>Example breakdown for {@code \"$$$$$${a}\"}: <ul> <li>{@code $$} &rarr; {@code $} (first escape pair)</li> <li>{@code $$} &rarr; {@code $} (second escape pair)</li> <li>{@code $${a}} &rarr; {@code ${a}} (third pair escapes the variable prefix)</li> </ul> Result: {@code \"$$$$${a}\"} </p>"
   },
